> Why augmentations and shortest blocking phases work

A matching M contains no two edges incident to the same vertex. An augmenting
path alternates edges outside and inside M, starting and ending at free
vertices. With q forward unmatched edges it has q-1 matched edges, so its
graph-edge length is 2q-1. Taking the symmetric difference flips these statuses
and gives

$$|M\mathbin{\triangle}P|=|M|+q-(q-1)=|M|+1.$$
\alttext(An augmenting path adds one more unmatched edge than it removes matched edges, increasing cardinality by one.)

If a larger matching exists, its symmetric difference with M decomposes into
alternating paths and cycles; at least one path has one more edge from the
larger matching, and is augmenting for M. Conversely any augmentation enlarges
M. Thus absence of an augmenting path characterizes maximum cardinality. Maximum
does not mean unique, weighted-optimal or preferred by every applicant.

BFS starts simultaneously from every free left vertex. A left distance counts
matched-right-to-left hops. An edge to a free right vertex at left depth d gives
q=d+1 forward pairs. The kernel retains the minimum such q and stops expanding
left labels at or beyond that cutoff. DFS follows increasing left distances and
accepts a free right endpoint only at that minimum q. Per-phase adjacency
cursors avoid rescanning an edge; a heap-allocated explicit left stack replaces
recursive calls. Dead-end pruning changes working labels, while the trace keeps
the actual pre-DFS BFS labels.

Within a phase, the chosen shortest paths are vertex-disjoint and form a maximal
collection: another disjoint shortest path would remain searchable. After all
are flipped, the shortest augmenting-path length strictly increases. The
standard phase argument gives O(sqrt(V)) successful phases: short lengths can
increase only O(sqrt(V)) times before reaching that scale, and then the
symmetric-difference decomposition with an optimum bounds the remaining number
of missing pairs by O(sqrt(V)). Each phase costs O(E+V) with the current-edge
representation. For V at least1 the resulting bound is O((E+V)sqrt(V)), with
O(E+V) ordinary working/output storage. Including V matters when isolated
vertices are supplied; an empty graph with vertices still requires
initialization. Duplicate input entries count in E but cannot inflate
cardinality.

> Preserve the original five applicants and four jobs

Keep the original eight eligibility edges, including the applicant who has only
job 0 available. The algorithm fills all four jobs and leaves one applicant
free. Its ordered choices are unchanged for this fixture. The grey lines in the
native plot are eligible pairs; thick blue lines are the returned matching. The
axes keep separate left and right index spaces.

The terminal unsuccessful BFS also yields a certificate. Let Z_L and Z_R be its
alternating reachable vertices, starting at free left vertices. Then

$$C=(L\setminus Z_L)\cup Z_R.$$
\alttext(A vertex cover consists of unreachable left vertices together with reachable right vertices from the terminal alternating search.)

Every edge is covered: an edge from a reachable left vertex enters a reachable
right vertex, while every other edge already meets L minus Z_L. No free right
vertex is reachable at termination. Each matched edge contributes exactly one
endpoint to C, and unmatched left vertices are reachable. Therefore |C|=|M|.
Every matching has size at most any vertex cover, so these two feasible objects
certify optimality without trusting a claimed phase count. The cover comes from
the actual terminal BFS, with no additional edge traversal.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hopcroft_karp_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hopcroft_karp_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto g = dm::algorithms::hopcroft_karp_example_graph();
    const auto r =
        dm::algorithms::hopcroft_karp_run(g.n_left, g.n_right, g.edges);
    std::print("| applicant | returned job |\n| ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t u = 0; u < g.n_left; ++u) {
        if (r.result.match_left[u] == dm::algorithms::kBipartiteUnmatched)
            std::print("| {} | free |\n", u);
        else
            std::print("| {} | {} |\n", u, r.result.match_left[u]);
    }
    std::print("\nMatching size {}; cover left {}; cover right {}; cover size {}; successful phases {}; BFS " "calls including terminal {}.\n",
               r.result.size, r.cover_left, r.cover_right,
               r.cover_left.size() + r.cover_right.size(), r.augmenting_phases,
               r.work.bfs_calls);
    dm::algorithms::hopcroft_karp_graph_plot(g, r.result,
                                             "Original eligibility graph: four jobs filled").save_svg("build/hopcroft_karp_original.svg");
    for (auto[u, v] : g.edges)
        if (std::find(r.cover_left.begin(), r.cover_left.end(), u) == r.cover_left.end() &&
            std::find(r.cover_right.begin(), r.cover_right.end(), v) ==
            r.cover_right.end())
            throw std::runtime_error("uncovered edge");
    if (r.result.size != 4 || r.cover_left.size() + r.cover_right.size() != 4)
        throw std::runtime_error("original certificate");
}
