> Validation and primary reference

Native ASan/UBSan tests compare all 65536 four-by-four simple graphs with an
independent bitmask matching oracle. They independently reconstruct BFS
distances, verify that every producing path is shortest, vertex-disjoint within
its phase and alternating before flipping, rebuild both orientation arrays,
check capture counts/work bounds and verify every edge against an equal-size
cover. Additional controls cover the cutoff regression, duplicates/order, empty
sides, exact/one-short policies, validation priority, deep ownership, public
fixtures/plot validation and the 20000-vertex-per-side chain. Six existing tests
include 500 random comparisons with Kuhn matching. Frozen comparisons retain
maximum cardinalities and the original fixture while explicitly recording
changed tie choices. An independent Python oracle additionally checks 1547
ordered, reversed, duplicated, fixed and chain graphs: 3754 actual paths, 10197
BFS labels, bitmask optima and terminal covers agree. Documentation examples,
representative hovers, real MEP execution, all inspected native figures and both
result/image exports are required before retiring the legacy report.

The primary source is [J. E. Hopcroft and R. M. Karp, An n^(5/2) Algorithm for
Maximum Matchings in Bipartite
Graphs](https://web.eecs.umich.edu/~pettie/matching/Hopcroft-Karp-bipartite-matching.pdf),
SIAM Journal on Computing 2(4), 225–231, 1973, [DOI
10.1137/0202019](https://doi.org/10.1137/0202019). Its shortest/disjoint-path
phase argument underlies the method. The supplied-input implementation
additionally permits isolated vertices and repeated edge entries and uses an
iterative current-edge DFS. The report's fixed eligibility graphs and synthetic
chains are not application data or historical runtime measurements from that
paper.
